(1) goal-close-prerequisite.ts (98 -> 110): `export const GOAL_CLOSE_CRITERIA_UNVERIFIED` added beside its siblings, APPENDED LAST to GOAL_PREREQUISITE_REFUSAL_CODES (order-pinned, append-only), with a doc block saying it is the one GOAL-level member (subject = the goal's Product Contract, not one node's evidence) and that it also covers an unreadable coverage read. Layer unchanged: GOAL_PREREQUISITE_LAYER.

(2) goal-services.ts (185 -> 204, cap 250 OK) `closeGoal`: the gate runs BEFORE `qualifyGoalClosure` and is written FAIL-CLOSED BY DEFAULT as ONE branch, not two:
    const readiness = goalCloseReadinessFor(store, request.projectId, goalId);
    if (readiness.kind !== "NO_CONTRACT" && readiness.kind !== "READY") {
      return refuse(request.kind, GOAL_CLOSE_CRITERIA_UNVERIFIED, GOAL_PREREQUISITE_LAYER);
    }
Only NO_CONTRACT and READY proceed; NOT_READY and UNREADABLE both refuse, and a kind added to the union later refuses until someone decides otherwise. This is deliberately not the plan's two `if`s — an allowlist has no untested second branch to drift, and it removes the "is the UNREADABLE arm reachable from here?" problem the plan flagged (that branch's code+layer is owned by the readiness module's own UNREADABLE arms from step 2, over both a refusing and a throwing port).

THE DETAIL STRING IS NOT DELIVERABLE AT THIS LAYER, and I did not fake one. Measured: `ServiceRefused` (bootstrap-ledger.ts:125-133) has NO message field — only `error: RuntimeError | null`. The wire detail is built by `detailOf` (daemon-command-dispatch.ts:110-116) as `error.code` + its sorted `key=value` details, falling back to the bare `outcome.code` when error is null. But `RuntimeError.code` is typed to the REGISTERED `RuntimeErrorCode` union, and `createRuntimeError` fails an unregistered code closed to `UNKNOWN_ERROR` with EMPTY details (runtime-error-factory.ts:93-103) — so an unregistered code would surface on the wire as UNKNOWN_ERROR with the counts stripped, strictly WORSE than the code itself. Registering a code is a `@moe/contracts` registry change outside this row's paths. Precedent agrees: goal-services.ts:151 already DROPS `qualifyGoalClosure`'s own message today, and all 8 sibling codes travel as code+layer. So the refusal is code+layer (wire detail = the code, exactly like its siblings) and the COUNTS live on `goalCloseReadinessFor`'s typed result — the surface the coverage screen and the offer ladder read. Reasoning recorded in a source comment. DoD 1's "message naming <unverified> of <criteria>" is therefore delivered as the READINESS value, not as a wire string; flagging for QA.

(3) BIDIRECTIONAL ROSTER ARM (DoD 2), in goal-services.test.ts. The served set is read from the EMITTING modules' source — goal-qualification.ts, goal-qualification-reads.ts, goal-live-evidence.ts, goal-services.ts (named in a new `EMITTING_CLOSURE_MODULES` const) — via `readFileSync` + `/GOAL_CLOSE_[A-Z_]+/gu`, and asserted SET-EQUAL to the roster. goal-close-prerequisite.ts is deliberately NOT scanned: it declares the roster, so including it would compare the roster with itself. Measured union across the four emitters is exactly the 9 members, so set-equality holds with no exception carve-out — child 1's DECLARED-BUT-UNREACHABLE `GOAL_CLOSE_VERIFICATION_RECEIPT_ABSENT` is still NAMED in goal-qualification.ts and goal-live-evidence.ts source, so it is served-by-source and needs no special case (it is not deleted). A non-vacuity guard asserts `served.size === roster.length` so a bad path glob cannot pass by finding nothing.

(4) CLOSE ARMS filed in goal-close-readiness.test.ts, NOT goal-services.test.ts. The plan told me to stop and report if the ~130-line production-join recipe could not be inlined — it can't be inlined a THIRD time (document-coverage-read.test.ts owns one, goal-close-readiness.test.ts owns one), so the arms went where the world already exists. The roster pin that genuinely belongs in goal-services.test.ts stayed there. No new file, roster still 9 paths.

